// Practice quiz: Reading: REST design recap
// Minimum release, November 2026: author-checked, no independent review. Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: spring-boot/porting-and-configuration/rest-design-recap

::o1 naming::Which path follows REST naming conventions for one order? {
~/getOrder/42#Not quite. The verb repeats what the method already says.
=/orders/42#Correct. A plural noun and an id.
~/order-fetch?id\=42#Not quite. This names an action instead of a resource.
~/orders/42/get#Not quite. The method already says get.
}

::o2 safe methods::Which method is safe in the RFC 9110 sense? {
~POST#Not quite. POST requests processing that can change state.
=GET#Correct. It does not request a change on the server.
~PUT#Not quite. PUT changes the resource.
~DELETE#Not quite. DELETE removes it.
}

::o2 idempotent::What does idempotent mean for an HTTP method? {
~It never changes anything#Not quite. That is safe, not idempotent.
~It always returns the same status#Not quite. A repeated DELETE may return a different status.
~It can only be sent once#Not quite. It can be sent many times.
=Repeating it has the same intended effect as sending it once#Correct. The final state does not change with extra identical requests.
}

::o2 retry::A client times out on a POST that creates an order. What is the risk of blindly retrying? {
=It may create a second order#Correct. POST is not idempotent by default.
~The server rejects any retry automatically#Not quite. Nothing does this by default.
~There is no risk#Not quite. The first request may have succeeded.
~The data is deleted#Not quite. A POST creating orders does not delete them.
}

::o3 consistent errors::Why use one JSON error shape across an API? {
~It makes the server faster#Not quite. Speed is not the goal.
~It removes the need for status codes#Not quite. Status codes still carry the outcome.
=Clients can handle every failure with the same code#Correct. Consistency removes per-endpoint special cases.
~HTTP requires it#Not quite. HTTP does not define your body shape.
}

